  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 AM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nToday and Tonight...\n\nThe cold front responsible for showers and storms overnight and\ninto this morning will be shifting east across the area today, \nclearing the Pine Belt by early afternoon. In its wake, cooler and\ndrier air associated with building high pressure will signal an \nend to rain chances and the period of warmth and humidity we have \nrecently experienced. Highs today will be a bit lower than \nyesterday for most, topping out in the lower 70s F northwest to \nnear 80 F in the southeast. Lows tonight, however, will be much \nchillier, dropping down into the 40s F for most to near 50 F in \nthe Pine Belt. \n\nThursday through early next week...\n\nWhile the pattern will be substantially more suppressed heading\ninto the weekend with high pressure and continental air largely in\ncontrol, an active southern stream jet will provide for an\nopportunity for increased cloudiness and perhaps an isolated\nshower or two through early next week. Overall, though, most\nlocations will stay dry and those that do see a brief shower won't\nsee much. The next 7 days will likely be dry and cool as overnight\nlows average 5 to 10 F below climate normals for this time of year.\n\n\nLows temperatures at times will flirt with the mid to lower 30s F,\nparticularly in some of the typically cooler spots. This could \nresult in some chance for a light frost though most places likely\nmanage to stay above freezing. The coldest night appears to come\nSunday night before we begin a slow moderating trend (lows \"only\"\ndown into the lower 40s F) into the middle of next week. \nRegardless, these near-freezing temperatures serve to remind that\nnow is a good time to begin practicing those winter safety \npractices. Ensuring plants, pets, and people are considered for as\nnights begin to more routinely threaten frost/freeze. /86/",
